    "OverallSummary": "This case involves Independent Financial Group, LLC (IFG) failing to establish, maintain, and enforce a supervisory system reasonably designed to supervise excessive trading and achieve compliance with Regulation Best Interest (Reg BI) and FINRA Rule 2111. Between July 2020 and December 2022, IFG failed to properly supervise a registered representative who excessively traded five customers' accounts, resulting in approximately $2.2 million in trading costs and $2.2 million in realized losses for these customers collectively.\n\nThe Care Obligation violations centered on excessive trading, which was identified through two primary metrics: (1) cost-to-equity ratios, with four of the five affected accounts having ratios between 20.3% and 27.1%, exceeding the 20% threshold generally indicating excessive trading; and (2) frequent in-and-out trading patterns that were inconsistent with the customers' investment profiles. This excessive trading was particularly egregious given the customers' demographics and investment objectives - three of the affected customers were retirees (ages 69, 77-78, and 88), with the oldest suffering from Alzheimer's disease. Most customers had moderate risk tolerances and investment objectives focused on capital preservation, income, or growth and income.\n\nIFG's supervisory failures included: (1) a flawed alert system that stopped generating alerts for six months after initial review; (2) failure to provide guidance to compliance staff on how to evaluate excessive trading; (3) failure to train supervisors on identifying patterns of excessive trading; and (4) failure to respond appropriately to red flags, such as accounts repeatedly appearing on excessive trading reports. The firm also violated FINRA Rules 8210 and 2010 by providing late and incomplete responses to FINRA's requests for information related to its investigation.\n\nFor these violations, IFG was censured, fined $500,000, and required to undertake remediation of its supervisory systems. All affected customers have either received restitution or are expected to receive full restitution through a separate agreement."
